    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 252243\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n343 PM PDT Sun Oct 25 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* Quiet and seasonable weather conditions through at least Wednesday.\n\n* Chances of precipitation, cooler temperatures, and gusty winds\n  return to the region late-week into the weekend.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...Today through Saturday.\n\nZonal-to-northwesterly flow aloft will result in generally quiet and \nseasonable weather through the first half of the work week. \nConditions will remain dry and temperatures will hover within a few \ndegrees of seasonal normals. A trough will push through the \nIntermountain West on Monday that will increase south-southwesterly \nwinds across the southern Great Basin, with speeds generally between \n15 and 25 mph. On Tuesday and Wednesday, expect similar wind speeds \nalong the Colorado River Valley with wave heights between 1 and 1.5 \nfeet at Lake Mohave and Lake Havasu. Otherwise, winds will remain \ngenerally light with afternoon breezes. \n\nThis weekend, a longwave trough will dominate the weather pattern \nand will return precipitation chances to the Desert Southwest late-\nweek through the remainder of the forecast period. Several \nshortwaves embedded in the aforementioned flow aloft will help guide \nanomalous subtropical moisture into the region, with forecast PWATs \nover 200 percent of normal for this time of year across our southern \nzones. The closed low associated with the longwave trough will ride \nalong the California coast as the weekend progresses, eventually \nweakening into an open wave as it moves inland and across \nsoutheastern California, southern Nevada, and northwestern Arizona. \nThat said, there remains a substantial amount of uncertainty \nregarding timing of the weather system, trajectory of the moisture \nflux, and strength of the low as it moves inland. Minor changes in \nthe trajectory of the moisture flux (southwesterly vs. southerly) \nwould result in a wide range of variance in precipitation amounts \nacross the forecast area. Continue to check back to the forecast as \nwe move through the work week if you have outdoor plans for \nHalloween weekend.\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 00Z Forecast Package...Winds\nwill remain light and follow typical daily directional trends \ninto the overnight hours, becoming light and variable early \ntomorrow morning. Winds will remain light, settling from the east \nlate tomorrow morning. VFR conditions will persist with increasing\nhigh cloud cover overnight, decreasing tomorrow afternoon.\n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast\nCalifornia...For the 00Z Forecast Package...Winds across the \nregion will remain relatively light, 12 knots or less, and will \nfollow typical, daily directional trends through the TAF period. \nWinds may become light and variable at times as winds transition. \nHigh clouds AOA 25 kft will increase from west to east overnight, \nclearing out during the afternoon tomorrow.\n\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Soulat\nAVIATION...Stessman\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
